  "id": "COMPOUND:14884 - PROTEIN:5841",
  "verbalization": "Borapetoside D is implicated in the interaction with A-431 through multiple pathways, with the most direct link established via the target Hepatocyte. This cell type is targeted by both Borapetoside D and a compound known as (2R,3R,4S,5S,6R)-2-[[(2R,3S,4S,5R,6R)-5-[(2S,3R,4R,5S,6R)-3,4-dihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)-5-[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxyoxan-2-yl]oxy-6-[(2S,3R,6R)-6-[(3S,8S,9S,10R,13R,14S,17R)-3-[(2R,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3-[(2S,3R,4R,5S,6R)-3,4-dihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)-5-[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxyoxan-2-yl]oxy-4,5-dihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxy-10,13-dimethyl-2,3,4,7,8,9,11,12,14,15,16,17-dodecahydro-1H-cyclopenta[a]phenanthren-17-yl]-2,3-dimethylheptoxy]-3,4-dihydroxyoxan-2-yl]methoxy]-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxane-3,4,5-triol, which also targets A-431. The second pathway involves (R)-b-(Boc-amino)-2,5-difluorobenzenebutanoic acid, another compound that targets A-431. These findings suggest that Borapetoside D may exert its effects through a mechanism that involves the Hepatocyte, potentially modulating pathways that lead to the inhibition of A-431. The evidence is consistent with a mechanism involving the Hepatocyte, but the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
